  "narrative": "I assessed all newly delivered discovery results under the stated sign convention: a positive signed estimate supports the registered hypothesis direction, and a negative signed estimate supports the opposite direction. For clinical significance, I used absolute comparator-scale means, approximated from the supplied outcome distributions, as the reference for a 10% relative difference. For most dependency scores centered near -0.25, this threshold is about 0.025 score units; for outcomes with means near zero, such as USP7 and NFE2L2, the percentage threshold is scale-sensitive, but absolute score differences still provide useful context. All delivered cells satisfied the 20-observation minimum.\n\nThe strongest accepted discoveries are the canonical synthetic-lethalities in this dataset: MSI-high cells show much stronger WRN dependency (R5; signed +0.790, CI 0.760 to 0.819), and SMARCA4-loss cells show much stronger SMARCA2 dependency (R6; signed +0.784, CI 0.765 to 0.803). These intervals are very tight relative to zero and far exceed the 10% relative-difference benchmark. I accept them as dataset-supported associations, while noting they are not causal claims and generalizability beyond this lung-lineage dataset is uncertain.\n\nThe KEAP1-loss hypothesis for stronger NFE2L2 dependency (H8, direction -1) is decisively contradicted. R8 has signed estimate -0.796 (CI -0.816 to -0.776), which corresponds to a raw exposed-minus-comparator difference in the opposite direction: KEAP1-loss cells have much less negative, i.e. weaker, NFE2L2 dependency. This is scientifically interpretable if constitutive NRF2 pathway activation reduces dependence on NFE2L2, but the original claim should be rejected. I registered a reversed KEAP1-loss/NFE2L2 proposal as a refinement of H8. I did not request validation for the original reversed-direction claim because the original direction is closed and the new proposal needs analysis under the new direction.\n\nH3, EGFR amplification versus EGFR dependency, is the key borderline canonical result: signed +0.0252 with CI -0.00025 to +0.0507. The point estimate is about 10% of the comparator mean, but the interval just includes zero and the exposed group is relatively small (n=175). Because multiplicity from 12 discovery tests makes borderline results fragile, I left it unresolved but active and requested independent validation for H3. If validation remains near or above the clinical threshold with an interval excluding zero, it could become a stronger association; otherwise the dataset evidence is too weak to accept.\n\nMost other canonical hypotheses showed small or uncertain contrasts. H1 KRAS-mutant versus KRAS dependency was modest (signed +0.0137, CI -0.0038 to +0.0312); the upper bound crosses the clinical threshold, so I kept it unresolved and registered a co-driver-restricted follow-up excluding BRAF V600E and EGFR amplification. H2 BRAF V600E versus BRAF dependency had a point in the opposite direction (signed -0.0165, CI -0.0448 to +0.0117) and did not support the original hypothesis; I deferred it pending better stratification. H4 ERBB2 amplification versus ERBB2 dependency was essentially null at the point estimate (signed +0.0015), though the interval still allows a clinically meaningful effect in either direction because the exposed group is small; it remains unresolved but deferred.\n\nTP53-loss hypotheses for weaker MDM2 (H7) and RPL11 (H12) dependencies are inconsistent. H7 has a very small positive point (signed +0.0088) but an interval upper bound above the clinical threshold, so it is unresolved but deferred. H12 has a near-zero point (signed +0.0037) and an interval upper bound below the approximate 10% threshold, so I reject it as not clinically meaningful in this dataset. H16 KRAS mutation versus RIT1 dependency (direction +1) was small and its upper bound was below the approximate clinical threshold, so it was also rejected.\n\nH9 MSI-high versus DHX9 dependency and H10 MYC amplification versus USP7 dependency remain unresolved and deferred. H9 is underpowered relative to the huge WRN MSI effect and does not support DHX9 dependency. H10 has a larger point relative to the near-zero USP7 mean, but it is imprecise and includes clinically meaningful evidence in both directions, so it is not acceptable as a dataset conclusion. H11, H13, H14, and H15 were deferred as untested but mechanistically broader or less canonical hypotheses.\n\nCritical limitations: these are unadjusted Welch comparisons in one dataset, all within lung lineage, without causal interpretation. Technical factors such as CRISPR library, batch, culture conditions, co-mutations, and omics availability could confound some associations, though QC and cell counts are adequate. Strong effects H5 and H6 are robust to multiple testing, whereas H3 is multiplicity-fragile. Independent validation is therefore requested for H3, and follow-up proposals focus on specificity and replication of the accepted strong effects plus a co-driver-restricted KRAS follow-up.",
